Explanation:
Step-by-Step Solution:
1. Statutory Text: Section 48, Republic Act No. 9003 (Prohibited Acts):
 'Prohibited Acts. — The following acts are prohibited:
 (1) Littering, throwing, dumping of waste matters in public places, such as roads, sidewalks, canals, esteros or parks, and establishment, or causing or permitting the same;
 (2) Undertaking activities, or operating, collecting or transporting equipment in violation of sanitation standards and other requirements or any permit issued pursuant to this Act;
 (3) The OPEN BURNING OF SOLID WASTE;
 (4) CAUSING OR PERMITTING THE COLLECTION OF NON-SEGREGATED OR UNSORTED WASTE;
 (5) SQUATTING IN OPEN DUMPS AND LANDFILLS;
 (6) Open dumping, burying of biodegradable or non-biodegradable loads in flood-prone areas;
 (7) Unauthorized removal of recyclable materials intended for collection by authorized persons...'
2. Penalties (Section 49):
 - Fines from PHP 300 to PHP 1,000 and 1-15 days community service for littering, escalating to PHP 500,000 fines and 6 years imprisonment for commercial/industrial violations.
3. Option A is the verbatim enumeration of statutory prohibited acts.

Common Trap: Thinking garbage haulers can collect mixed trash; HAULING OR COLLECTING NON-SEGREGATED WASTE IS AN EXPLICIT CRIME under Section 48(4) ('No Segregation, No Collection' policy).
Exam Tip: Prohibited Acts under RA 9003 (Sec. 48) = Littering + Open Burning + Collecting Non-Segregated Waste + Squatting in Landfills + Open Dumping.

Step-by-Step Solution:
1. Landmark Statutory Amendment: Republic Act No. 11898 (Extended Producer Responsibility Act of 2022, lapsed into law July 23, 2022, amending RA 9003):
2. Core Principles of EPR Law:
 - 1. Obliged Enterprises (OEs): Large enterprises with total assets exceeding PHP 100 Million that manufacture or import plastic packaging for consumer goods.
 - 2. Life-Cycle Accountability: Manufacturers are legally responsible for the post-consumer life cycle of their plastic packaging (sachets, pouches, bottles, polystyrene, labels).
 - 3. Progressive Recovery & Diversion Targets (Section 44-F):
 - 2023: 20% plastic footpr∫ recovery;
 - 2024: 40% plastic footpr∫ recovery;
 - 2025: 50% plastic footpr∫ recovery;
 - 2026: 60% plastic footpr∫ recovery;
 - 2027: 70% plastic footpr∫ recovery;
 - 2028 and beyond: EIGHTY PERCENT (80%) ANNUAL PLASTIC RECOVERY / DIVERSION.
 - 4. Stiff Fines: Fines of PHP 5,000,000 to PHP 20,000,000 and revocation of business permits for non-compliance.
3. Option A is the verbatim statutory requirement under the EPR Act.

Common Trap: Believing plastic management is only the consumer's fault; the EPR LAW (RA 11898) MAKES PRODUCERS LEGALLY RESPONSIBLE for recovering their plastic footpr∫ up to 80% by 2028.
Exam Tip: EPR Law (RA 11898 / RA 9003) = Extended Producer Responsibility; Obliged large enterprises must recover and divert 80% of their plastic packaging footpr∫ by 2028.

Step-by-Step Solution:
1. Statutory Text: Section 12 & 16, Republic Act No. 9003:
 'City and Municipal Solid Waste Management Plans. — The province, city or municipality, through its local solid waste management boards, SHALL PREPARE ITS RESPECTIVE 10-YEAR SOLID WASTE MANAGEMENT PLAN CONSISTENT WITH THE NATIONAL SOLID WASTE MANAGEMENT FRAMEWORK: Provided, That the plan shall be reviewed and updated every two (2) years...
 All local government solid waste management plans shall be subjected to the review and APPROVAL OF THE NATIONAL SOLID WASTE MANAGEMENT COMMISSION (NSWMC).'
2. Mandatory Elements of the 10-Year Plan (Section 17):
 - (1) Waste Characterization (WACS - Waste Analysis and Characterization Study);
 - (2) Source Reduction & Segregation Strategy;
 - (3) Collection and Transfer Strategy (Schedule of segregated pickup);
 - (4) Recycling & Composting Program (Barangay MRF inventory);
 - (5) Final Disposal Strategy (Sanitary Landfill site/access);
 - (6) Information, Education, and Communication (IEC) Campaign.
3. Option A is the verbatim statutory requirement.

Common Trap: Thinking local plans last 1 year; solid waste plans are 10-YEAR COMPREHENSIVE STRATEGIC PLANS approved by the NSWMC.
Exam Tip: RA 9003, Sec. 12 = Every LGU must formulate a 10-YEAR SOLID WASTE MANAGEMENT PLAN approved by the NSWMC and updated every 2 years.
